{"questions":[{"id":"board_6941a2acf0c106d14860787c","_marksId":"6941a2acf0c106d14860787c","q":"<p>Find the adjoint of the matrix `[(2,-2),(4,3)]`.</p>","options":[],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>`A = [(2,-2),(4,3)]`</p>\n<p>adj (A) = `[(d,-b),(-c,a)]`</p>\n<p>`A = [(2,-2),(4,3)]`</p>\n<p>`adj(A) = [(3,2),(-4,2)]`</p>\n<p>`adj ([(2,-2),(4,3)]) = [(3,2),(-4,2)]`</p><br><br>","subject":"Mathematics & Statistics","chapter":"Matrices","exam":"HSC","examName":"HSC Board","_bank":"board","questionType":"subjective","source":"HSC Boards - 2025","paperSource":"HSC Boards - 2025","paperDate":"2025-03-01T09:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"board_6941a2acf0c106d148607850","_marksId":"6941a2acf0c106d148607850","q":"<p><strong>Check whether the following matrix is invertible or not:</strong></p>\n<p>`[(cos theta, sin theta),(-sin theta, cos theta)]`</p>","options":[],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>Let A = `[(cos theta, sin theta),(-sin theta, cos theta)]`</p>\n<p>Then, |A| = `|(cos theta, sin theta),(-sin theta, cos theta)|`</p>\n<p>= cos<sup>2</sup>θ + sin<sup>2</sup>θ</p>\n<p>= 1 ≠ 0</p>\n<p>∴ A is a non-singular matrix.</p>\n<p>Hence, A is invertible matrix.</p><br><br>","subject":"Mathematics & Statistics","chapter":"Matrices","exam":"HSC","examName":"HSC Board","_bank":"board","questionType":"subjective","source":"HSC Boards - 2024","paperSource":"HSC Boards - 2024","paperDate":"2024-03-01T09:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"board_6941a2acf0c106d148607824","_marksId":"6941a2acf0c106d148607824","q":"<p>If A = `[(\"x\",0,0),(0,\"y\",0),(0,0,\"z\")]` is a non-singular matrix, then find A<sup>−1</sup> by using elementary row transformations. Hence, find the inverse of `[(2,0,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,-1)]`</p>","options":[],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>|A| = `[(\"x\",0,0),(0,\"y\",0),(0,0,\"z\")]`</p>\n<p>= x(yz) − 0 + 0</p>\n<p>= xyz ≠ 0</p>\n<p>Since A is a non-singular matrix, A<sup>−1</sup> exists.</p>\n<p>Consider, AA<sup>−1</sup> = I</p>\n<p>∴ `[(\"x\",0,0),(0,\"y\",0),(0,0,\"z\")] \"A\"^-1 = [(1,0,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,1)]`</p>\n<p>By `(1/\"x\") \"R\"_1, (1/\"y\")\"R\"_2` and `(1/\"z\")\"R\"_3,` we get,</p>\n<p>`[(1,0,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,1)] \"A\"^-1 = [(1/\"x\",0,0),(0,1/\"y\",0),(0,0,1/\"z\")]`</p>\n<p>∴ A<sup>−1</sup> = `[(1/\"x\",0,0),(0,1/\"y\",0),(0,0,1/\"z\")]`</p>\n<p>Comparing `[(2,0,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,-1)]` with `[(\"x\",0,0),(0,\"y\",0),(0,0,\"z\")]`</p>\n<p>we get, x = 2, y = 1, z = - 1</p>\n<p>∴ `1/x = 1/2, 1/y = 1/1 = 1, 1/z = 1/-1 = - 1`</p>\n<p>Hence, the inverse of</p>\n<p>`[(2,0,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,-1)]  \"is\" [(1/2,0,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,-1)]`</p><br><br>","subject":"Mathematics & Statistics","chapter":"Matrices","exam":"HSC","examName":"HSC Board","_bank":"board","questionType":"subjective","source":"HSC Boards - 2023","paperSource":"HSC Boards - 2023","paperDate":"2023-03-01T09:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"board_6941a2abf0c106d1486077f8","_marksId":"6941a2abf0c106d1486077f8","q":"<p>Find the cofactors of the elements of the matrix</p>\n<p>`[(-1, 2),(-3, 4)]`</p>","options":[],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>Given, matrix is `[(-1, 2),(-3, 4)]`</p>\n<p>Here a<sub>11</sub> = –1; ∴ N<sub>11</sub> = 4 and A<sub>11</sub> = (–1)<sup>1+1</sup> (4) = 4</p>\n<p>a<sub>12</sub> = 2; ∴ N<sub>12</sub> = –3 and A<sub>12</sub> = (–1)<sup>1+2</sup> (–3) = 3</p>\n<p>a<sub>21</sub> = –3; ∴ N<sub>21</sub> = 2 and A<sub>21</sub> = (–1)<sup>2+1</sup> (2) = –2</p>\n<p>a<sub>22</sub> = 4; ∴ N<sub>22</sub> = –1 and A<sub>22</sub> = (–1)<sup>2+2</sup> (–1) = –1</p>\n<p>∴ The required cofactors are 4, 3, –2, –1.</p><br><br>","subject":"Mathematics & Statistics","chapter":"Matrices","exam":"HSC","examName":"HSC Board","_bank":"board","questionType":"subjective","source":"HSC Boards - 2022","paperSource":"HSC Boards - 2022","paperDate":"2022-03-01T09:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"board_6941a2abf0c106d14860779f","_marksId":"6941a2abf0c106d14860779f","q":"<p>If A = `[(2,-3),(4,1)]`, then adjoint of matrix A is ______.</p>","options":["<p>`[(1,3),(-4,2)]`</p>","<p>`[(1,-3),(-4,2)]`</p>","<p>`[(1,3),(4,-2)]`</p>","<p>`[(-1,-3),(-4,2)]`</p>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>If A = `[(2,-3),(4,1)]`, then adjoint of matrix A is `bbunderline([(1,3),(-4,2)])`.</p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>We are given a 2 × 2 matrix:</p>\n<p>`A = [(2, 3),(-4, 1)]`</p>\n<p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Formula for adjoint of a 2 × 2 matrix</p>\n<p>`A = [(a, b),(c, d)]`, the adjoint is: adj (A) = `[(d, -b),(-c, a)]`</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2: </strong>Apply to our matrix</p>\n<p>Given: </p>\n<p>`A = [(2, -3),(4, 1)]`</p>\n<p>So: </p>\n<ul>\n<li>a = 2</li>\n<li>b = −3</li>\n<li>c = 4</li>\n<li>d = 1</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Now compute the adjont: </p>\n<p>adj(A) = `[(1, 3),(-4, 2)]`</p><br><br>","subject":"Mathematics & Statistics","chapter":"Matrices","exam":"HSC","examName":"HSC Board","_bank":"board","questionType":"singleCorrect","source":"HSC Boards - 2018","paperSource":"HSC Boards - 2018","paperDate":"2018-03-01T09:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"board_6941a2abf0c106d148607778","_marksId":"6941a2abf0c106d148607778","q":"<p>The inverse of the matrix `[[-1,5],[-3,2]]` is _______</p>","options":["<p>1/13`[[2,-5],[3,-1]]`</p>","<p>1/13 `[[-1,5],[-3,2]]`</p>","<p>1/13 `[[-1,-3],[5,2]]`</p>","<p>1/13 `[[1,5],[3,-2]]`</p>"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p><strong>(A)</strong> 1/13`[[2,-5],[3,-1]]`</p>\n<p>|A| = - 2 + 15 = 13</p>\n<p>`A^-1 = (\"adj\" A)/|A| = ([[2,-5],[3,-1]])/13`</p>\n<p>`A^-1=1/13[[2,-5],[3,-1]]`</p><br><br>","subject":"Mathematics & Statistics","chapter":"Matrices","exam":"HSC","examName":"HSC Board","_bank":"board","questionType":"singleCorrect","source":"HSC Boards - 2017","paperSource":"HSC Boards - 2017","paperDate":"2017-03-01T09:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"board_6941a2acf0c106d148607893","_marksId":"6941a2acf0c106d148607893","q":"<p>Find the inverse of A = `[(\"cos\" theta, -\"sin\" theta, 0),(\"sin\" theta, \"cos\" theta, 0),(0,0,1)]` by elementary row transformations.</p>","options":[],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>|A| = `|(\"cos\" theta, -\"sin\" theta, 0),(\"sin\" theta, \"cos\" theta, 0),(0,0,1)|`</p>\n<p>= cos θ(cos θ – 0) + sin θ(sin θ – 0) + 0</p>\n<p>= cos<sup>2</sup>θ + sin<sup>2</sup>θ</p>\n<p>= 1 ≠ 0</p>\n<p>∴ A<sup>-1</sup> exists.</p>\n<p>Consider AA<sup>–1</sup> = I</p>\n<p>∴ `[(\"cos\" theta, -\"sin\" theta, 0),(\"sin\" theta, \"cos\" theta, 0),(0,0,1)] \"A\"^-1 = [(1,0,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,1)]`</p>\n<p>By cos θ × R<sub>1</sub>, we get,</p>\n<p>`[(\"cos\"^2theta, -\"sin\"theta \"cos\"theta,0),(\"sin\"theta, \"cos\"theta, 0),(0,0,1)] \"A\"^-1 = [(\"cos\"theta,0,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,1)]`</p>\n<p>By R<sub>1</sub> + sin θ × R<sub>2</sub>, we get,</p>\n<p>`[(1,0,0),(\"sin\"theta,\"cos\"theta,0),(0,0,1)] \"A\"^-1 = [(\"cos\"theta,\"sin\"theta,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,1)]`</p>\n<p>By R<sub>2</sub> – sin θ × R<sub>1</sub>, we get,</p>\n<p>`[(1,0,0),(0,\"cos\"theta,0),(0,0,1)] \"A\"^-1 = [(\"cos\"theta,\"sin\"theta, 0),(-\"sin\"theta\"cos\"theta,\"cos\"^2theta,0),(0,0,1)]`</p>\n<p>By `(1/(\"cos\"theta)) xx \"R\"_2`, we get,</p>\n<p>`[(1,0,0),(0,1,0),(0,0,1)] \"A\"^-1 = [(\"cos\"theta,\"sin\"theta,0),(-\"sin\"theta,\"cos\"theta,0),(0,0,1)]`</p>\n<p>`∴ \"A\"^-1 = [(\"cos\"theta,\"sin\"theta,0),(-\"sin\"theta,\"cos\"theta,0),(0,0,1)]`</p><br><br>","subject":"Mathematics & Statistics","chapter":"Matrices","exam":"HSC","examName":"HSC Board","_bank":"board","questionType":"subjective","source":"HSC Boards - 2025","paperSource":"HSC Boards - 2025","paperDate":"2025-03-01T09:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"board_6941a2acf0c106d148607867","_marksId":"6941a2acf0c106d148607867","q":"<p><strong>Solve the following system of equations by the method of reduction:</strong></p>\n<p>x + y + z = 6, y + 3z = 11, x + z = 2y.</p>","options":[],"answer":0,"solution":"<p><strong>Given:</strong></p>\n<p>x + y + z = 6</p>\n<p>y + 3z = 11</p>\n<p>x + z = 2y</p>\n<p>`[(1, 1, 1), (0, 1, 3), (1, -2, 1)] [(x), (y), (z)] = [(6), (11), (0)]`</p>\n<p>using R<sub>3</sub> → R<sub>3</sub> – R<sub>1</sub></p>\n<p>we get `[(1, 1, 1), (0, 1, 3),(0, -3, 0)] [(x), (y), (z)] = [(6), (11), (-6)]`</p>\n<p>using R<sub>3</sub> → R<sub>3</sub> + 3R<sub>2</sub></p>\n<p>we get `[(1, 1, 1), (0, 1, 3),(0, 0, 9)] [(x), (y), (z)] = [(6), (11), (27)]`</p>\n<p>∴ `[(x + y + z),(0 + y + 3z),(0 + 0 + 9z)] = [(6), (11), (27)]`</p>\n<p>Equating, we get</p>\n<p>x + y + z = 6   ...(1)</p>\n<p>y + 3z = 11  ...(2)</p>\n<p>9z = 27     ...(3)</p>\n<p>z = `27/9`    ...[from eq. (3)]</p>\n<p>z = 3</p>\n<p>put this value of z in equation (2), we get</p>\n<p>y + 3(3) = 11</p>\n<p>y + 9 = 11</p>\n<p>y = 11 − 9</p>\n<p>y = 2</p>\n<p>put the values of y and z in equation (1), we get</p>\n<p>x + y + z = 6</p>\n<p>x + 2 + 3 = 6</p>\n<p>x + 5 = 6</p>\n<p>x = 6 − 5</p>\n<p>x = 1</p><br><br>","subject":"Mathematics & Statistics","chapter":"Matrices","exam":"HSC","examName":"HSC Board","_bank":"board","questionType":"subjective","source":"HSC Boards - 2024","paperSource":"HSC Boards - 2024","paperDate":"2024-03-01T09:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"board_6941a2acf0c106d14860783c","_marksId":"6941a2acf0c106d14860783c","q":"<p>If A = `[(1, 2),(3, 4)]` verify that A (adj A) = (adj A) A = |A| I</p>","options":[],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>For A = `[(1, 2),(3, 4)]`</p>\n<p>A<sub>11</sub> = (–1)<sup>1 + 1</sup> (4) = 4</p>\n<p>A<sub>12</sub> = (–1)<sup>1 + 2</sup> (3) = –3</p>\n<p>A<sub>21</sub> = (–1)<sup>2 + 1</sup> (2) = –2</p>\n<p>A<sub>22</sub> = (–1)<sup>2 + 2</sup> (1) = 1</p>\n<p>adj A = `[(A_11, A_21),(A_12, A_22)]`</p>\n<p>= `[(4, -2),(-3, 1)]`</p>\n<p>∴ A(adj A) = `[(1, 2),(3, 4)][(4, -2),(-3, 1)]`</p>\n<p>=  `[(4 - 6, -2 +2),(12 -12, -6 + 4)]`</p>\n<p>= `[(-2, 0),(0, -2)]`  ...(i)</p>\n<p>(adj A) . A = `[(4, -2),(-3, 1)][(1, 2),(3, 4)]`</p>\n<p>= `[(4 - 6, 8 - 8),(-3 + 3, -6 + 4)]`</p>\n<p>= `[(-2, 0),(0, -2)]`  ...(ii)</p>\n<p>and |A| I = `[(1, 2),(3, 4)][(1, 0),(0, 1)]` = 4 -6 = -2</p>\n<p>= `(-2) [(1, 0),(0, 1)]`</p>\n<p>= `[(-2, 0),(0, -2)]`  ...(iii)</p>\n<p>From (i), (ii) and (iii) we get,</p>\n<p>A(adj A) = (adj A) A = |A| I</p><br><br>","subject":"Mathematics & Statistics","chapter":"Matrices","exam":"HSC","examName":"HSC Board","_bank":"board","questionType":"subjective","source":"HSC Boards - 2023","paperSource":"HSC Boards - 2023","paperDate":"2023-03-01T09:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"board_6941a2abf0c106d148607810","_marksId":"6941a2abf0c106d148607810","q":"<p>Solve the following system of equations by the method of inversion.</p>\n<p>x – y + z = 4, 2x + y – 3z = 0, x + y + z = 2</p>","options":[],"answer":0,"solution":"<p><strong>Matrix form of the given system of equations is:</strong></p>\n<p>`[(1, -1, 1),(2, 1, -3),(1, 1, 1)][(x),(y),(z)] = [(4),(0),(2)]`</p>\n<p>This is of the form AX= 8, where</p>\n<p>A = `[(1, -1, 1),(2, 1, -3),(1, 1, 1)]`, X = `[(x),(y),(z)]` and B = `[(4),(0),(2)]`</p>\n<p>To determine X, we have to find A<sup>–1</sup>.</p>\n<p>|A| = `|(1, -1, 1),(2, 1, -3),(1, 1, 1)|` = 1(1 + 3) + 1(2 + 3) + 1(2 – 1)</p>\n<p>= 1(4) + 1(5) + 1(1)</p>\n<p>= 10 ≠ 0</p>\n<p>∴ A<sup>–1</sup> exists</p>\n<p>Consider AA<sup>–1</sup> = I</p>\n<p>`[(1, -1, 1),(2, 1, -3),(1, 1, 1)] A^-1 = [(1, 0, 0),(0, 1, 0),(0, 0, 1)]`</p>\n<p>Applying R<sub>2</sub> → R<sub>2</sub> – 2R<sub>1</sub> and R<sub>3</sub> → R<sub>3</sub> – R<sub>1</sub>, we get</p>\n<p>`[(1, -1, 1),(0, 3, -5),(0, 2, 0)] A^-1 = [(1, 0, 0),(-2, 1, 0),(-1, 0, 1)]`</p>\n<p>Applying R<sub>2</sub> → `1/3`(R<sub>2</sub>), we get</p>\n<p>`[(1, -1, 1),(0, 1, (-5)/3),(0, 2, 0)] A^-1 = [(1, 0, 0),((-2)/3, 1/3, 0),(-1, 0, 1)]`</p>\n<p>Applying R<sub>1</sub> → R<sub>1</sub> + R<sub>2</sub> and R<sub>3</sub> → R<sub>3</sub> – 2R<sub>2</sub>, we get</p>\n<p>`[(1, 0, (-2)/3),(0, 1, (-5)/3),(0, 0, 10/3)] A^-1 = [(1/3, 1/3, 0),((-2)/3, 1/3, 0),(1/3, (-2)/3, 1)]`</p>\n<p>Applying R<sub>1</sub> → `3/10`R<sub>3</sub>, we get</p>\n<p>`[(1, 0, (-2)/3),(0, 1, (-5)/3),(0, 0, 1)] A^-1 = [(1/3, 1/3, 0),((-2)/3, 1/3, 0),(1/10, (-1)/5, 3/10)]`</p>\n<p>Applying R<sub>1</sub> → R<sub>1</sub> + `(2/3)`R<sub>3</sub> and R<sub>2</sub> → R<sub>2</sub> + `5/3`R3, we get</p>\n<p>`[(1, 0, 0),(0, 1, 0),(0, 0, 1)] A^-1 = [(2/5, 1/5, 1/5),(-1/2, 0, 1/2),(1/10, (-1)/5, 3/10)]`</p>\n<p>∴ A<sup>–1</sup> = `1/10[(4, 2, 2),(-5, 0, 5),(1, -2, 3)]`</p>\n<p>Now, AX = B</p>\n<p>∴  A<sup>–1</sup>(AX) = A<sup>–1</sup>(B)</p>\n<p>∴ IX = A<sup>–1</sup>B</p>\n<p>∴ X = A<sup>–1</sup>B</p>\n<p>∴ X = `1/10 [(4, 2, 2),(-5, 0, 5),(1, -2, 3)][(4),(0),(2)]`</p>\n<p>∴ `[(x),(y),(z)] = 1/10 [(16 + 0 + 4),(-20 + 0 + 10),(4 - 0 + 6)]`</p>\n<p>= `1/10 [(20),(-10),(10)]`</p>\n<p>= `[(2),(-1),(1)]`</p>\n<p>Hence. by equality of matrices x = 2, y = – 1, z = 1.</p><br><br>","subject":"Mathematics & Statistics","chapter":"Matrices","exam":"HSC","examName":"HSC Board","_bank":"board","questionType":"subjective","source":"HSC Boards - 2022","paperSource":"HSC Boards - 2022","paperDate":"2022-03-01T09:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"board_6941a2abf0c106d1486077df","_marksId":"6941a2abf0c106d1486077df","q":"<p>If three numbers are added, their sum is 2. If two times the second number is subtracted from the sum of the first and third numbers, we get 8, and if three times the first number is added to the sum of the second and third numbers, we get 4. Find the numbers using matrices. </p>","options":[],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>Let the three numbers x , y , z.</p>\n<p>From given condition, we have</p>\n<p>x + y + z = 2  .......(1)</p>\n<p>x + z - 2y = 8 </p>\n<p>x - 2y + z = 8  ......(2)</p>\n<p>And </p>\n<p>3x + y + z = 4   .....(3)</p>\n<p>Given all equation can be written in matrix form as ,</p>\n<p>`[(1,1,1),(1,-2,1),(3,1,1)] [(x),(y),(z)] = [(2),(8),(4)]`</p>\n<p>Consider , AX = B</p>\n<p>On multiplying A<sup>-1</sup> both sides , we get</p>\n<p>X = A<sup>-1</sup> . B   ......(4)</p>\n<p>Now</p>\n<p>|A| = `|(1,1,1),(0,-3,0),(0,-2,-2)| [(x),(y),(z)] = [(2),(6),(-2)]`</p>\n<p>`|(x+,y+,z),(0 - ,3y ,+ 0),(0,-2,-2)|  = [(2),(6),(-2)]`</p>\n<p>By equality of matrices,</p>\n<p>x + y + z = 2 ……(1)</p>\n<p>-3y = 6 ……(2)</p>\n<p>– 2y – 2z = -2 ……..(3)</p>\n<p>From (2), y = -2</p>\n<p>Substituting y = -2 in (3), we get,</p>\n<p>-2(-2) – 2z = -2</p>\n<p>∴ -2z = -6</p>\n<p>∴ z = 3</p>\n<p>Substituting y = -2, z = 3 in (1), we get,</p>\n<p>x – 2 + 3 = 2</p>\n<p>∴ x = 1</p>\n<p>Hence, the required numbers are 1, -2 and 3.</p><br><br>","subject":"Mathematics & Statistics","chapter":"Matrices","exam":"HSC","examName":"HSC Board","_bank":"board","questionType":"subjective","source":"HSC Boards - 2019","paperSource":"HSC Boards - 2019","paperDate":"2019-03-01T09:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"board_6941a2abf0c106d1486077ac","_marksId":"6941a2abf0c106d1486077ac","q":"<p>Find the inverse of the matrix `A = [(1,2,-2),(-1,3,0),(0,-2,1)]`using elementary row transformations.</p>","options":[],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>`A = [(1,2,-2),(-1,3,0),(0,-2,1)]`</p>\n<p>= 1[3] - 2[-1] - 2[2]</p>\n<p>`= 3 + 2 - 4 = 1 != 0 =&gt; A^(-1)` exist </p>\n<p>we know</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fhsc_maharashtra%252F12%252Fpublic%252F6941afd2e3720f1c028af4f2.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fhsc_maharashtra%2F12%2Fpublic%2F6941afd2e3720f1c028af4f2.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fhsc_maharashtra%2F12%2Fpublic%2F6941afd2e3720f1c028af4f2.png?alt=media\"/></p><br><br>","subject":"Mathematics & Statistics","chapter":"Matrices","exam":"HSC","examName":"HSC Board","_bank":"board","questionType":"subjective","source":"HSC Boards - 2018","paperSource":"HSC Boards - 2018","paperDate":"2018-03-01T09:00:00.000Z"},{"id":"board_6941a2abf0c106d148607782","_marksId":"6941a2abf0c106d148607782","q":"<p>Solve the following equations by method of reduction: </p>\n<p>x-y + z = 4,</p>\n<p>2x + y - 3z = 0,</p>\n<p>x + y + z = 2</p>","options":[],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>x-y + z = 4,</p>\n<p>2x + y - 3z = 0,</p>\n<p>x + y + z = 2</p>\n<p>`[[1,-1,1],[2,1,-3],[1,1,1]][[x],[y],[z]]=[[4],[0],[2]]`</p>\n<p>`R_2-2R_1 and R_3-R_1`</p>\n<p>`[[1,-1,1],[0,3,-5],[0,2,0]][[x],[y],[z]]=[[4],[-8],[-2]]`</p>\n<p>`x-y+z=4`   ....(1)</p>\n<p>`3y-5z=-8` ....(2)</p>\n<p>`2y=-2`           ....(3)</p>\n<p>y=-1</p>\n<p>By equation (2)</p>\n<p>`–3 – 5z = – 8`<br/>`–5z = –5`<br/>`z = 1`</p>\n<p>By equation (1)</p>\n<p>`x + 1 + 1 = 4`<br/>`x = 2`<br/>Ans : `x = 2, y = –1, z = 1`</p><br><br>","subject":"Mathematics & Statistics","chapter":"Matrices","exam":"HSC","examName":"HSC Board","_bank":"board","questionType":"subjective","source":"HSC Boards - 2017","paperSource":"HSC Boards - 2017","paperDate":"2017-03-01T09:00:00.000Z"}]}